{"_type":"item","id":"019dc10364bf7f268c5c7dd07743c37f","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Plugin platform Tier 2: in-process GDScript backend","description":"The current plugin platform (DCR-1) defines plugins as out-of-process binaries speaking stdio MCP. This works for plugins that need crash isolation, native code, or non-Godot languages (Go, Python). It's overkill for simpler plugins: annotation-kind-only plugins, UI-only plugins (alternate file browser, theme switcher), workflow-wrapper plugins that just orchestrate existing Minerva tools.\n\nA second tier would run plugins as in-process GDScript — direct access to SingletonObject, providers, editors; no capability bridging; no IPC overhead. Manifest would use a typed `backend` field discriminating kinds (today's `stdio` + future `in_process_gdscript`).\n\n**Deferred because:**\n- Tier 1 covers all currently-planned consumers (CAD via DCR-3, Presentation via DCR-4, PCB migration via future DCR).\n- Adds a second lifecycle model, trust surface, and loader path — real platform complexity.\n- Better to let a concrete consumer motivate the design than pre-build.\n\n**Unblocks when:** a real plugin consumer has needs that Tier 1 serves poorly *and* don't warrant a binary backend.\n\n**Design considerations to settle when this is picked up:**\n- Lifecycle supervisor — does PluginManager extend, or does Tier 2 use a lighter path?\n- Trust model — in-process means zero process isolation; match existing policy (user-approved on install, audit-logged) or add additional restrictions (sandboxed GDScript environment)?\n- Crash handling — GDScript exceptions crash the plugin; do they propagate and crash Minerva? What recovery?\n- Hot-reload story — simpler or more complex than Tier 1?\n- MCP tool registration path — direct into MinervaMCPServer, or still via a plugin-scoped namespace with proxying?\n- Manifest shape compatibility — whether to reserve the typed `backend` field in DCR-1 now (forward-compat) or only when picking this up (breaking change to existing manifests at that point).\n\n**Context:** Discussed 2026-04-24 in plugin platform design conversation. Motivating question: \"what about Godot as a back-end for a plugin?\" Recommendation at the time: defer implementation; see `Docs/Plugin-platform-thought.md` §2 for current Tier 1 design. Related: DCR-1 (out-of-process tier). User asked the question; coordinator recommended defer; user concurred.","created_at":"2026-04-24T19:41:58","updated_at":"2026-04-24T19:41:58","priority":4,"tags":["deferred","future","plugin-system","tier-2"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc125834f72e987ffdaf88fc152a7","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Scene-panel progress-notification routing (plugin → scene)","description":"Plugin Python worker emits `{method:\"progress\", params:{id, phase, fraction}}` notifications over stdio. Go plugin forwards to Minerva. Minerva's scene broker needs a dedicated progress channel (separate from normal ipc_channels so manifests don't have to declare it explicitly per plugin). The scene receives `progress(request_id, phase, fraction)` and renders however it chooses (progress bar, spinner, status label). Driven by Go-python-bridge design Q6 resolution (wire now, not later).\n\nDoD:\n- Progress notifications pass through the scene broker without plugins needing to declare them as ipc_channels.\n- Scene root receives `on_progress(request_id, phase: String, fraction: float)` callback.\n- Multiple concurrent requests identified by request_id.\n- Test: a mock plugin emits 10 progress events during a 2-second operation; the scene receives them in order.","created_at":"2026-04-24T20:19:14","updated_at":"2026-04-24T23:02:05","tags":["platform-addition","plugin-system","progress"],"parent":"minerva:019dc054b6267e70987927a316e363dd"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc1259c977380b41ba54b13e57162","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"CAD panel: surface progress notifications from worker","description":"Consumer of the platform-side progress-notification routing task. When the CAD Go plugin's Python worker emits progress (e.g., during OCCT tessellation or deviation sampling), the CAD panel displays a non-modal progress indicator — status label plus fraction bar. Cancellation is deadline-only in v1 (no user cancel button yet); the indicator just informs.\n\nDoD:\n- CAD panel receives progress events via scene broker's progress channel.\n- Long `evaluate`, `export`, `deviation` calls surface phase + fraction to the panel.\n- Progress indicator clears on completion or error.\n- Test: triggering a slow evaluate shows progress crossing at least one phase boundary.","created_at":"2026-04-24T20:19:20","updated_at":"2026-04-24T20:19:20","tags":["cad","progress","ui"],"parent":"minerva:019dc0552d6a774dae54e01190e0029f"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019dc127985a7bbf91051f009226ec40","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Plan: Plugin runtime dispatch (base primitives + multi-runtime extension)","description":"Sequenced tasks. Base primitives (items 1-3 from original DCR scope) come first because they unblock simple hosted/binary plugins immediately. Multi-runtime extension (items 4-9) builds on them and lands when CAD or PCB needs the dispatch layer — which is when plugins want local-or-hosted failover. Tasks linked as children of this plan.","created_at":"2026-04-24T20:21:30","updated_at":"2026-04-24T20:21:30","tags":["plan","plugin-system","runtime"],"parent":"minerva:019da41823517154afe359ec70d513bd"}
