{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0030","type":"rca","status":"detected","title":"5-Why: Critical notifications dropped during LLM streaming","description":"5-Why Root Cause Analysis for DKT-0004: Message queue overflow dropping critical notifications.\n\n## Symptom\nClient never receives `awaiting_user` notification after review completes. Job appears stuck. 2058 message drops observed in one session.\n\n## Why 1: Why does the client miss the awaiting_user notification?\nBecause the pub/sub notification is fire-and-forget — if the message is dropped anywhere in the pipeline, there is no retry or acknowledgment mechanism.\n\n## Why 2: Why is the message dropped?\nBecause the server-side asyncio.Queue (50K maxsize) overflows during LLM streaming. When the queue is full, `put_nowait()` silently drops new messages with only a print() statement (service.py:1153-1155).\n\n## Why 3: Why does the queue overflow during LLM streaming?\nBecause LLM token streaming generates hundreds of `response_chunk` events per second via `_stream_llm_traffic()` (autocoder.py:5216-5342), and a single `_message_handler_worker` processes them sequentially. During review phase with 3 agents streaming simultaneously, throughput exceeds the worker's processing capacity.\n\n## Why 4: Why can't the worker keep up with streaming volume?\nTwo factors:\n(a) **Single worker bottleneck**: Only one asyncio task (`_message_handler_worker`, service.py:1108-1130) processes ALL incoming messages — streaming chunks, status updates, storage responses, everything.\n(b) **No priority differentiation**: Critical status messages (awaiting_user) enter the same FIFO queue as high-volume streaming data, with no priority mechanism to ensure delivery.\n\n## Why 5: Why is there no priority mechanism or multi-worker architecture?\nThe message queue was designed as a simple decoupling layer (receive loop → handler) and was scaled by increasing maxsize from default to 50K. The architecture assumes all messages have equal importance and that a single worker can keep pace. No backpressure, priority queuing, or guaranteed delivery was designed for the system.\n\n## Contributing Factors (Client-Side)\n- WebSocket buffer conflict: global 16MB limit vs per-socket 32MB setting (core_client.gd:89-90 vs 185-187)\n- Single-frame packet processing in _process() — no backpressure\n- Signal connection race condition in AutocoderManager subscription setup\n- Event buffer cap of 200 events with FIFO eviction\n- No client-side polling fallback for missed critical notifications\n\n## Root Cause\nThe message bus lacks a guaranteed delivery mechanism for critical state-transition notifications. The system treats all messages equally in a single FIFO queue, and high-volume LLM streaming saturates the queue, displacing low-volume but high-importance status messages.\n\n## Potential Fix Directions\n1. **Priority queue**: Separate queues or priority levels for status vs streaming messages\n2. **Guaranteed delivery**: ACK/retry mechanism for critical state transitions\n3. **Client-side polling**: Periodic session state poll as fallback for missed notifications\n4. **Backpressure**: Rate-limit or batch streaming events when queue pressure is high\n5. **Multi-worker**: Multiple workers or dedicated worker for critical message types","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:13:53","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:48","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["autocoder","message-bus","notifications","queue","rca"],"parent":"DKT-0004"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0031","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Improve review agent timeout handling and defaults","description":"Design Change Request for DKT-0007: Review agent timeout improvements.\n\n## Current State\n- Per-agent `timeout_seconds` is already configurable (30-600s range) — feature exists\n- Default is 120s (review_models.py:37) — too low for verbose models\n- On timeout: all accumulated findings DISCARDED, returns empty ReviewResult\n- Retry loop can attempt up to 78 HTTP requests per agent before giving up\n- No client-side UI to set timeout or see per-agent progress\n\n## Proposed Changes\n\n### A. Increase Default Timeout\n- Change default from 120s → 180s in `review_models.py:37`\n- Provides breathing room for standard models without needing per-agent config\n\n### B. Model-Tier-Based Defaults\n- Add model-aware timeout defaults based on model identifier:\n  - Fast models (haiku, flash, nano): 120s\n  - Standard models (sonnet, gpt-4o): 180s\n  - Verbose models (opus, gpt-5.2, o1): 300s\n- Applied when `timeout_seconds` is not explicitly set by user\n\n### C. Preserve Partial Results on Timeout\n- When final JSON synthesis times out, preserve accumulated tool findings\n- Return partial ReviewResult with `issues` extracted from tool call history\n- Add `partial: true` flag to indicate incomplete review\n\n### D. Adaptive Final JSON Timeout\n- Increase timeout for final JSON call to `timeout_seconds × 1.5`\n- Rationale: synthesis step is the most critical and often the longest\n\n### E. Client-Side UX (Minerva)\n- Expose `timeout_seconds` field in ReviewAgentManager agent editor UI\n- Show per-agent progress indicator during review phase\n- Display timeout-specific error messages (not generic \"Generation error\")\n\n### F. Fix HTTP Client Default\n- Change `review_executor.py:33` from `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=120.0)` to `timeout=None`\n- Let per-request timeout be the sole authority","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:13:53","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:52","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["autocoder","dcr","review","timeout"],"parent":"DKT-0007"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0032","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Remove legacy queue gate — enable direct-await message loop for all services","description":"## Goal\nEliminate the 50K asyncio.Queue bottleneck by switching all services from `_legacy_message_loop()` to `_message_loop()`.\n\n## Current State\n- `service.py:758` gates on `network_config.enabled AND connection_retry_loop`\n- `connection_retry_loop` is imported from `minerva.network_resilience` which **does not exist**\n- So ALL services fall through to `_single_connection_attempt()` → `_legacy_message_loop()` → 50K queue + single worker\n\n## What To Do\n\n### Option A: Remove the gate (minimal change)\nIn `service.py`, modify `start()` to always call `_start_with_resilience()` or restructure so the new `_message_loop()` is used even without `connection_retry_loop`. The resilience retry loop is a separate concern from which message loop to use.\n\nConcretely:\n1. In `_single_connection_attempt()` (line 863), change `_legacy_message_loop(websocket)` → `_message_loop(websocket)`\n2. Keep `_legacy_message_loop` for backward compatibility but mark deprecated\n3. Remove the 50K queue entirely from the active code path\n\n### Option B: Create minimal network_resilience module\nCreate `minerva/network_resilience.py` with a simple `connection_retry_loop` async generator that yields reconnected WebSockets. This unlocks `_start_with_resilience()` which already uses `_message_loop()`.\n\n### Safety Check\nThe direct-await `_message_loop()` blocks on `await self.text_message_received()`. Verify that `text_message_received()` in the autocoder orchestrator returns quickly — it should, because all long-running handlers (generate, plan, review, answer-question) are already spawned via `_spawn_background_handler()` (service.py:1184-1199). Only lightweight handlers like list-sessions and cleanup are awaited directly.\n\n### Test\n- Run a full autocoder session (plan → generate → review → awaiting_user)\n- Verify no message drops under load\n- Verify status notifications arrive at client without delay\n\n## Files\n- `infrastructure/minerva-services/minerva/service.py` — lines 758-762, 827-868, 1042-1090, 1131-1168\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/autocoder.py` — lines 1417-1519 (text_message_received handler routing)","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:22:14","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:52","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["autocoder","message-bus","minerva-services","queue"],"parent":"DKT-0030"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0033","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add client-side session state polling as notification fallback","description":"## Goal\nAdd a periodic polling fallback in the Minerva Godot client so that if a critical pub/sub notification (like `awaiting_user`) is missed, the client still discovers the state change within a few seconds.\n\n## Why\nEven after fixing the server-side queue, pub/sub is inherently best-effort. A defense-in-depth approach requires the client to not rely solely on one-shot notifications for critical state transitions.\n\n## What To Do\n\n### Server Side (already partially done)\nThe `/session-state/<session_id>` endpoint was added to `openrouter-proxy` in Bug 019 fix. However, the full session review state (`awaiting_user`, `ai_review`, etc.) lives in the orchestrator's storage, not the proxy.\n\nOptions:\n1. **Add `/session/status` endpoint to orchestrator** — new REST-like request topic that returns current `review_state` and `status` for a session. The orchestrator already has `autocoder/session/info` handler (autocoder.py:1440) — extend it or add a lightweight status-only variant.\n2. **Use existing session/info** — the client can send an `autocoder/session/info` request via Core pub/sub and get the full session state back.\n\n### Client Side (Minerva Godot)\nIn `AutocoderManager.gd`, add a timer-based poll:\n\n1. When a job is submitted (state = `in_review` or `generating`), start a `Timer` node with 5-10s interval\n2. On each tick, send `autocoder/session/info` request for the active session\n3. Compare returned `review_state` with local `AutocoderLogStore.review_state`\n4. If server says `awaiting_user` but client hasn't received the notification → trigger the approval card display\n5. Stop polling when job reaches terminal state (`awaiting_user`, `complete`, `error`)\n\n### Files\n- `Minerva/src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/AutocoderManager.gd` — add poll timer + handler\n- `Minerva/src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/AutocoderLogStore.gd` — compare states\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/autocoder.py` — ensure session/info returns review_state (verify existing handler at line 1440)","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:22:29","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:53","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":2,"severity":2,"tags":["autocoder","godot","minerva-client","notifications"],"parent":"DKT-0030"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0034","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Add drop counter and structured logging for queue overflow","description":"## Goal\nReplace the silent `print()` on queue overflow with proper structured logging and a drop counter, so we have operational visibility into message loss even if the queue architecture remains.\n\n## Current State\n`service.py:1154-1155`:\n```python\nexcept asyncio.QueueFull:\n    print(f\"[{self.SERVICE_ID}] Warning: Message queue full, dropping message\")\n```\nNo counter, no logger, no message type info. 2058 drops went unnoticed until user saw delayed notifications.\n\n## What To Do\n1. Add a `self._queue_drop_count` counter (int) initialized in `_legacy_message_loop()`\n2. Replace `print()` with `logger.warning()` including:\n   - Drop count (cumulative)\n   - Current queue size (`self._message_queue.qsize()`)\n   - Dropped message type (`data.get('cmd')`, `data.get('topic')`)\n   - Whether the dropped message was a status notification (flag critical drops)\n3. Log a summary every 100 drops (avoid log spam during sustained overflow)\n4. Optionally expose drop count in service health/status endpoint\n\n## Files\n- `infrastructure/minerva-services/minerva/service.py` — lines 1131-1168\n\n## Note\nThis is an observability improvement. If the queue is eliminated (DKT-0032), this becomes moot for the direct-await path, but still valuable if any service falls back to the legacy path.","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:22:39","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:53","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["autocoder","message-bus","minerva-services","observability"],"parent":"DKT-0030"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0035","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Fix Godot WebSocket buffer settings conflict","description":"## Goal\nResolve the conflicting WebSocket buffer settings in the Minerva Godot client that may silently cap buffers at 16MB instead of 32MB.\n\n## Current State\n`core_client.gd`:\n- Lines 89-90 (`_ready()`): Global project settings set to 16384 KB (16 MB)\n  ```gdscript\n  ProjectSettings.set_setting(\"network/limits/websocket/max_in_buffer_kb\", 16384)\n  ProjectSettings.set_setting(\"network/limits/websocket/max_out_buffer_kb\", 16384)\n  ```\n- Lines 185-187 (`connect_to_core()`): Per-socket buffers set to 32 MB\n  ```gdscript\n  _client.inbound_buffer_size = 32 * 1024 * 1024\n  _client.outbound_buffer_size = 32 * 1024 * 1024\n  ```\n\nGodot may enforce the SMALLER of the two limits, making the 32MB per-socket setting ineffective.\n\n## What To Do\n1. **Research**: Verify which limit Godot 4.x actually enforces when both are set (project settings vs per-socket). Check Godot docs/source.\n2. **Align settings**: Either raise the project setting to match (32768 KB) or remove the project setting entirely and rely on per-socket config.\n3. **Consider reducing**: 32 MB buffers may be overkill. With the queue fix (DKT-0032) and polling fallback (DKT-0033), buffer pressure should drop. Evaluate whether 8-16 MB is sufficient.\n\n## Files\n- `Minerva/src/Scripts/Services/Providers/Core/core_client.gd` — lines 89-90, 185-187","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:22:48","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:53","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["godot","minerva-client","websocket"],"parent":"DKT-0030"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0036","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Increase default review timeout and add model-tier defaults","description":"## Goal\nRaise the default review agent timeout from 120s to 180s and add model-aware defaults so verbose models get longer timeouts automatically.\n\n## Current State\n- `review_models.py:37`: `timeout_seconds: int = 120` — default for all models\n- `review_executor.py:33`: `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=120.0)` — client-level default (overridden per-request but still a confusing secondary source)\n- Per-agent override already works via `timeout_seconds` field (range 30-600s)\n\n## What To Do\n\n### A. Raise default\nIn `review_models.py:37`, change `timeout_seconds: int = 120` → `timeout_seconds: int = 180`\n\n### B. Model-tier defaults\nIn `review_executor.py`, when building `ReviewModelConfig` for custom agents (lines 2422-2438), if the agent does NOT specify `timeout_seconds`, apply a model-tier default based on the model identifier:\n```python\nMODEL_TIER_TIMEOUTS = {\n    \"fast\": 120,     # haiku, flash, nano, gpt-4o-mini\n    \"standard\": 180, # sonnet, gpt-4o, gemini-pro\n    \"verbose\": 300,  # opus, gpt-5.2, o1, o3\n}\n```\nMatch by substring in model string (e.g., `\"haiku\" in model` → fast tier).\n\n### C. Fix HTTP client default\nIn `review_executor.py:33`, change `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=120.0)` → `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=None)`. Per-request timeout is already set explicitly; the client default just causes confusion.\n\n### D. Adaptive final JSON timeout\nIn `review_executor.py`, for the final JSON synthesis call (lines 1313-1395), use `timeout=model_config.timeout_seconds * 1.5` instead of the base timeout. The synthesis step is the most critical and often needs extra time after extensive tool exploration.\n\n## Test\n- Verify default agents get 180s timeout\n- Verify a model with \"opus\" gets 300s\n- Verify a model with \"haiku\" gets 120s\n- Verify explicit `timeout_seconds` override still takes precedence\n- Verify final JSON call gets 1.5x timeout\n\n## Files\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/review_models.py` — line 37\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/review_executor.py` — lines 33, 937, 1347, 2422-2438","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:23:07","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:54","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["autocoder","review","timeout"],"parent":"DKT-0031"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0037","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Preserve partial review results on timeout","description":"## Goal\nWhen a review agent times out on the final JSON synthesis, preserve the findings accumulated during tool iterations rather than discarding everything.\n\n## Current State\n`review_executor.py:1618-1628`: On any error (including timeout), returns:\n```python\nReviewResult(model_id=..., approved=False, summary=f\"Review failed: {error}\", issues=[], error=error)\n```\n- `issues=[]` — ALL findings lost\n- If the agent spent 14+ tool calls reading files and running tests, then timed out on final JSON → all that work is wasted\n\n## What To Do\n\n### A. Track findings during tool iterations\nIn `_call_llm()`, maintain a `partial_findings` list that accumulates as tool calls complete:\n- After each `response_complete` with tool calls, extract any structured findings from the assistant's messages\n- Look for patterns like \"Issue found:\", file references, error descriptions\n- Store raw assistant text from each tool iteration as fallback\n\n### B. Build partial result on timeout\nWhen the final JSON call times out (lines 1313-1395), instead of returning empty `issues=[]`:\n1. Attempt to parse any structured findings from `partial_findings`\n2. If structured parse fails, include the raw assistant text as a single \"partial review\" issue\n3. Set `partial: true` flag on the ReviewResult\n4. Set `summary` to \"Review partially completed — final synthesis timed out. N findings extracted from tool iterations.\"\n\n### C. Surface partial results to user\nIn the orchestrator (`autocoder.py`), when a ReviewResult has `partial=True`:\n- Include findings in the review summary sent to client\n- Mark the agent's review as \"partial\" in the approval card\n- Let the user see what the agent found before it timed out\n\n## Files\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/review_executor.py` — lines 890-1395 (_call_llm), 1618-1628 (error result)\n- `infrastructure/services/autocoder-orchestrator/autocoder.py` — review result processing","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:23:20","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:54","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":2,"severity":3,"tags":["autocoder","data-loss","review","timeout"],"parent":"DKT-0031"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0038","type":"work_item","status":"backlog","title":"Expose timeout_seconds in Minerva review agent editor UI","description":"## Goal\nLet users configure `timeout_seconds` per review agent through the Minerva agent editor UI, so they can increase timeouts for verbose models without needing to know about the backend API.\n\n## Current State\n- Backend supports `timeout_seconds` (30-600s) per agent — fully functional\n- Minerva's `ReviewAgentManager.gd` (lines 128-252) shows agent cards with: name, model, tools toggle, prompt preview, order controls\n- **No timeout field** in the agent editor UI\n- Users have no way to discover or set this parameter through the UI\n\n## What To Do\n\n### Agent Card / Editor\nIn the agent create/edit form (ReviewAgentManager.gd):\n1. Add a `SpinBox` or `HSlider` for \"LLM Timeout (seconds)\"\n2. Range: 30-600, step: 30, default: 180 (or show \"auto\" for model-tier default)\n3. Place below the model selector — timeout is model-dependent so proximity helps\n4. Tooltip: \"Max seconds per LLM request. Increase for verbose models (Opus, GPT-5.2). Default: 180s.\"\n\n### Agent Card Display\nIn `_build_agent_card()`, add a compact timeout indicator:\n- Show current timeout value (e.g., \"180s\" or \"auto\") alongside the model name\n- Only show if non-default, to avoid clutter\n\n### Data Flow\n- On save: include `timeout_seconds` in the agent dict sent to orchestrator\n- On load: read `timeout_seconds` from agent config, fall back to placeholder \"auto\"\n\n## Files\n- `Minerva/src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/ReviewAgentManager.gd` — agent editor form + card display\n- `Minerva/src/Scripts/UI/Controls/Autocoder/manager/ReviewAgentManager.tscn` — scene layout (add SpinBox node)","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:23:32","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:55","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":3,"severity":3,"tags":["godot","minerva-client","review","timeout","ux"],"parent":"DKT-0031"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0039","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Docket allows creation of blank bugs/items.","description":"Simply hitting New --> item creates and saves a blank item. This is unwanted behavior. I want to explicitely \"save changes\" when making new items.","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:27:50","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:55","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["Docket"],"repro_steps":"1. Open docket\n2. Hit New -> Bug (really any item)\n3. Don't hit save changes.\n4. Hit \"<Back\"\n\nExpected results:\nNo new item created\n\nActual result:\nblank new item created, saved.\n"}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0040","type":"bug","status":"new","title":"Don't know where items save when multiple dockets open","description":"Bug is bigger than title. I need to be able to specifiy which docket I'm working with when making new items.","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:30:39","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:56","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["Docket"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0041","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Improve Query","description":"Query workflow has issues that make it hard to use\n- Need an [Any] value for type. Sometimes I wnt to see all Pri 1 items, regardless of item type.\n- Need a way to query for parents.\n-- I have a parent work item (or bug, I forget), and children have been created. I can't query for those children.\n- Need \"parented\" items to have a \"show children\" in details somehow.\n- MCP query calls should update UI if I'm not actively typing (or something like that). \n-- If I'm just sitting in query view, not doing something with bugs, and I ask the agents for a query -- I can't see the query results, as it return the query results to the agent, but doesn't update my view.","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:36:24","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:58","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["Docket"]}
{"_type":"item","id":"DKT-0042","type":"dcr","status":"proposed","title":"Docket needs test type","description":"Docket needs a \"Test\" type that indicates a (hopefully automated) test to run against things.\n- Description of the test case so LLMs can choose what tests to run when making changes.\n- Test setup instructions\n-- Conda Envs, needed libs, needed resources -- can even be a \"setup\" script or something.\n- Test steps\n- Expected results\n\nWhen a test is run, it needs to go into the \"runs\" of the test case\n- What date/ time did it run?\n- What was the result?\n-- If it failed or warned -- why? What went wrong?\n","created_at":"2026-03-02T18:48:37","updated_at":"2026-03-03T00:12:59","assigned_to":"ClaudeCode","priority":1,"severity":1,"tags":["Docket"]}
